{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"### [3/3] PRIOR ART + THE RECLAIM DECISION\n\nPRIOR ART (cited): crates.io index (31 squashes since 2018; RFC 2789 says \"shallow clones and history squashing offer only temporary relief\"; snapshot branches MOVED to a separate repo in 2022 because \"the server still has to consider their contents whenever Cargo asks for master\" - 1.73 GB live vs 6.94 GB archive), Homebrew 4.0 (JSON over CDN; earlier hard-banned shallow clones at GitHub's request, generating a `critical`-labelled message rewrite in 4 days and a thread locked \"too heated\"), CocoaPods (shallow clones CAUSED GitHub CPU rate-limiting; still carries a 1.3 GB repo it can never shrink; exit is to freeze and archive Dec 2026), Go module proxy (immutable content-addressed zips; git demoted to a server-side detail). git-filter-repo itself refuses to run outside a fresh clone and directs you to publish a NEW URL and have everyone reclone.\nKEY DISQUALIFIER FOUND: every squash case had exactly ONE writer. rebar's tickets branch is multi-writer, converged by --allow-unrelated-histories. No prior art exists for a squash on a merge-replicated multi-writer branch.\n\nRECLAIM: initially dropped on that basis, then RE-ADMITTED by operator decision under an explicit quiescence protocol (paused schedulers + confirmed zero sessions = single-writer for the duration, which IS the precondition prior art required).\n\nMECHANICS PROVEN on the real branch (not theoretical): `git fast-export --no-data | filter | git fast-import` => 1,172,586,762 -> 98,803,422 bytes (8.4%), all 49,888 commits and 2,661 merges preserved, HEAD tree byte-identical except the intended paths, ~30s. No new dependency (git-filter-repo NOT required, and is worse here because it prunes empty commits and would reshape the DAG the ancestry-based trust model walks).\n\nTRUST MODEL VERIFIED SAFE: verify-identity uses git only for ancestry and reads bodies from the working tree; no literal commit SHA is bound anywhere EXCEPT rebar.toml:115 `enforce_since` (the only one; `verify.opcert_enforce_since` is unset). With require_authenticated=false, verify_authorship.py:531-550 returns early and never even reads enforce_since - so no CI deadlock and the re-pin lands normally. Plan-review and completion attestations also survive: they pin CODE SHAs and file-content hashes, never tickets SHAs.\n\nBLOCKERS FOUND AND DESIGNED AROUND: (1) the keystone guard must sit in BOTH union sites - inside sync._union_merge (NOT before sync.py:96, which would block the ff-adopt that a behind clone needs and wedge it forever) AND before push.py:203/:229; (2) it goes in the EXISTING .store-compat.json (compat.py ignores unknown keys - verified - so adding `epoch` cannot crash or fail-close an older binary) with expand/contract: reader ships first, value appears only in the rewrite commit; (3) remote tag pre-heal-a118-20260710T005736Z pins 22,317 commits / 63 MB and MUST be deleted or the reclaim lands at 167 MB not 94 MiB; (4) .bridge_state.bak-retarget/prev_snapshot.json is 33,025,572 bytes sitting in the tree AT HEAD - delete before the strip; (5) the re-add set must be derived DYNAMICALLY (A2-1 adds a 4th file, get_rotation.json)."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785372000449756001,"uuid":"5be2f067-caf4-4717-b565-4516b6bfe430"}